Polar coordinates ask two totally different questions than Cartesian does:
- How far from the center? (that's r, the radius)
- At what angle? (that's θ, theta—the direction you're pointing)

So instead of (x, y), a point becomes (r, θ). Same location, different way of describing it. {{viz:diagram-02}}

**Quick example:** the Cartesian point (3, 4) is exactly the same spot as the polar point (5, 53°)—5 units from the center, tilted 53° up from the x-axis. Two languages, one address.

**So when's polar actually better?** Any time something moves around a center point. Think orbits, spinning wheels, ripples spreading out from a stone dropped in water. {{viz:anim-01}}

Here's a picture that makes it click: a radar screen. You're at the center, and every blip shows up as "this far away, in this direction." That's polar in action—way more natural than forcing everything into (x, y) when the whole scene is circular. {{viz:chart-01}}

Cartesian says "go sideways, then up." Polar says "go this far, in this direction." Same destination, just a different set of directions to get there—and picking the right one can turn a messy problem into a simple one.
